    "conventions": "Most people carry a given name, a family name and, on documents, a patronymic from the father's name. Kazakh family names usually come from a grandfather's or father's name with a Russian-style ending: Akhmetov for a man, Akhmetova for a woman. Some families now use the Kazakh forms instead: -uly for a son and -kyzy for a daughter (Nurlan Serikuly). Russian, Tatar and Uyghur families follow the same -ov and -ova pattern. Koreans of Kazakhstan, the Koryo-saram, mostly have Russian given names with Korean family names in their Russian spelling: Tsoi, Ten, Pak, Kim. Each group's family list is the men's form; family_f gives the women's form where it differs.",
